# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Mode-based indexing {#mode-based-indexing}"
msgstr "Indexation par modes {#mode-based-indexing}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "All operator representations refer to the indices that their terms act upon as **modes**. A mode is simply an index identifying a fermionic degree of freedom in your system. The mapping from physical degrees of freedom (such as spatial orbitals, spin states, or other quantum numbers) to mode indices is left to the user, enabling maximum flexibility.\n"
msgstr "Toutes les représentations d'opérateurs désignent les indices sur lesquels agissent leurs termes comme des **modes**. Un mode est simplement un indice qui identifie un degré de liberté fermionique de ton système. La correspondance entre les degrés de liberté physiques (comme les orbitales spatiales, les états de spin ou d'autres nombres quantiques) et les indices de modes est laissée à l'utilisateur, ce qui offre une flexibilité maximale.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This abstraction is also present in the [`qiskit_fermions.circuit`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit#module-qiskit_fermions.circuit \"qiskit_fermions.circuit\") module, where the [`FermionicCircuit`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit-fermionic-circuit#qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit \"qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit\") operates on a register of fermionic modes. In both the operator and circuit representations, modes provide a consistent, algebra-independent way to specify which degrees of freedom participate in a given operation.\n"
msgstr "Cette abstraction est également présente dans le module [`qiskit_fermions.circuit`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit#module-qiskit_fermions.circuit \"qiskit_fermions.circuit\"), où le [`FermionicCircuit`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit-fermionic-circuit#qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit \"qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit\") opère sur un registre de modes fermioniques. Dans les représentations d'opérateurs comme de circuits, les modes offrent un moyen cohérent et indépendant de l'algèbre de préciser quels degrés de liberté participent à une opération donnée.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Important\" type=\"danger\">\n"
"  **Current implementations use spinless modes**: All operator representations currently provided by this module treat modes as spinless fermionic degrees of freedom. This means if your system has both spin-up and spin-down electrons or fermions, you must explicitly map them to distinct modes (for example, modes 0-3 for spin-up of four spatial orbitals, modes 4-7 for spin-down, or any other convention you choose).\n"
msgstr ""
"<Admonition title=\"Important\" type=\"danger\">\n"
"  **Les implémentations actuelles utilisent des modes sans spin** : toutes les représentations d'opérateurs actuellement fournies par ce module traitent les modes comme des degrés de liberté fermioniques sans spin. Cela signifie que si ton système contient à la fois des électrons ou des fermions de spin haut et de spin bas, tu dois les associer explicitement à des modes distincts (par exemple, les modes 0 à 3 pour le spin haut de quatre orbitales spatiales, les modes 4 à 7 pour le spin bas, ou toute autre convention de ton choix).\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  This design keeps the core representations simple and general while avoiding imposing a specific spin-ordering convention. Utility modules like [`qiskit_fermions.operators.library`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-library#module-qiskit_fermions.operators.library \"qiskit_fermions.operators.library\") provide convenience functions (for example, [`FCIDump.from_file()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-library-fci-dump#qiskit_fermions.operators.library.FCIDump.from_file \"qiskit_fermions.operators.library.FCIDump.from_file\")) that handle such mappings for you when loading electronic structure data.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""
"  Cette conception garde les représentations de base simples et générales tout en évitant d'imposer une convention particulière d'ordonnancement des spins. Les modules utilitaires comme [`qiskit_fermions.operators.library`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-library#module-qiskit_fermions.operators.library \"qiskit_fermions.operators.library\") fournissent des fonctions pratiques (par exemple, [`FCIDump.from_file()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-library-fci-dump#qiskit_fermions.operators.library.FCIDump.from_file \"qiskit_fermions.operators.library.FCIDump.from_file\")) qui gèrent ces correspondances à ta place lors du chargement de données de structure électronique.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Hint\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  As the package evolves, spinful operator representations might be added that natively support spin degrees of freedom in their data model. These will be clearly distinguished from the current spinless implementations and will coexist with them in the module.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""
"<Admonition title=\"Astuce\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  Au fil de l'évolution du paquet, des représentations d'opérateurs avec spin pourraient être ajoutées, prenant en charge nativement les degrés de liberté de spin dans leur modèle de données. Elles seront clairement distinguées des implémentations actuelles sans spin et coexisteront avec elles dans le module.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"term-grouping\" />\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Term grouping and commutation relationships {#term-grouping-and-commutation-relationships}"
msgstr "Regroupement des termes et relations de commutation {#term-grouping-and-commutation-relationships}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Like the coefficients and mode indices, operators can optionally store a **groups array** that associates each term with a group index. By integrating grouping directly into the operator representation as part of the sparse data structure, grouping information naturally travels with the operator through transformations. This enables systematic structure usage, whether from physical properties, algebraic relationships, or problem-specific symmetries. The structured information can then be used in downstream operations like circuit synthesis and decomposition by using methods like [`OperatorTrait.split_out_groups()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.split_out_groups \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.split_out_groups\").\n"
msgstr "Comme les coefficients et les indices de modes, les opérateurs peuvent éventuellement stocker un **tableau de groupes** qui associe chaque terme à un indice de groupe. En intégrant le regroupement directement dans la représentation de l'opérateur, au sein de la structure de données creuse, l'information de regroupement accompagne naturellement l'opérateur au fil des transformations. Cela permet d'exploiter systématiquement la structure, qu'elle provienne de propriétés physiques, de relations algébriques ou de symétries propres au problème. Cette information structurée peut ensuite servir dans des opérations en aval comme la synthèse et la décomposition de circuits, grâce à des méthodes comme [`OperatorTrait.split_out_groups()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.split_out_groups \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.split_out_groups\").\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "For detailed guidance on how to group operator terms in your workflows, see the [grouping](grouping#grouping-explanation) guide.\n"
msgstr "Pour des indications détaillées sur la façon de regrouper les termes d'opérateurs dans tes workflows, consulte le guide [regroupement](grouping#grouping-explanation).\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    ```python\n"
"    >>> from qiskit_fermions.operators import MajoranaOperator, gamma\n"
"    >>> op = MajoranaOperator.from_dict({\n"
"    ...     (gamma(0, False),): 1.0,\n"
"    ...     (gamma(1, False),): 1.0,\n"
"    ...     (gamma(2, False), gamma(3, False)): 1.0\n"
"    ... })\n"
"    >>> # Assign group indices to terms\n"
"    >>> op.groups = [0, 0, 1]\n"
"    >>> # Partition operator by groups\n"
"    >>> grouped_ops = op.split_out_groups()\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Create operator with 3 terms\n"
"    QkComplex64 coeffs[3] = {{1.0, 0.0}, {1.0, 0.0}, {1.0, 0.0}};\n"
"    uint32_t modes[4] = {0, 1, 2, 3};\n"
"    uint32_t boundaries[4] = {0, 1, 2, 4};\n"
"    QfMajoranaOperator *op = qf_maj_op_new(3, 4, coeffs, modes, boundaries);\n"
msgstr ""
"    // Create operator with 3 terms\n"
"    QkComplex64 coeffs[3] = {{1.0, 0.0}, {1.0, 0.0}, {1.0, 0.0}};\n"
"    uint32_t modes[4] = {0, 1, 2, 3};\n"
"    uint32_t boundaries[4] = {0, 1, 2, 4};\n"
"    QfMajoranaOperator *op = qf_maj_op_new(3, 4, coeffs, modes, boundaries);\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Assign grouping information\n"
"    uint32_t groups[3] = {0, 0, 1};\n"
"    qf_maj_op_set_groups(op, groups, 3);\n"
msgstr ""
"    // Assign grouping information\n"
"    uint32_t groups[3] = {0, 0, 1};\n"
"    qf_maj_op_set_groups(op, groups, 3);\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Partition operator by groups\n"
"    QfMajoranaOperator *grouped_ops[2];\n"
"    qf_maj_op_split_out_groups(op, NULL, 0, grouped_ops);\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
"</Tabs>\n"
msgstr ""
"    // Partition operator by groups\n"
"    QfMajoranaOperator *grouped_ops[2];\n"
"    qf_maj_op_split_out_groups(op, NULL, 0, grouped_ops);\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
"</Tabs>\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"id3\" />\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Arithmetic and mathematical operations {#arithmetic-and-mathematical-operations}"
msgstr "Opérations arithmétiques et mathématiques {#arithmetic-and-mathematical-operations}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "All operators implement the [`OperatorTrait`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait\") protocol, which provides a unified set of operations across different operator types. This ensures that code written for one operator representation works uniformly with others. See [`qiskit_fermions.protocols`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/protocols#module-qiskit_fermions.protocols \"qiskit_fermions.protocols\") for how this protocol relates to the others this package defines.\n"
msgstr "Tous les opérateurs implémentent le protocole [`OperatorTrait`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait\"), qui fournit un ensemble unifié d'opérations pour les différents types d'opérateurs. Cela garantit que le code écrit pour une représentation d'opérateur fonctionne de la même manière avec les autres. Consulte [`qiskit_fermions.protocols`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/protocols#module-qiskit_fermions.protocols \"qiskit_fermions.protocols\") pour voir comment ce protocole se rapporte aux autres définis par ce paquet.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The protocol includes arithmetic operations (such as addition, multiplication, and composition), structural operations (term iteration, mode support analysis, relabeling), mathematical functions (normal ordering, simplification, equivalence checking), and more. See the [`OperatorTrait`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait\") documentation for a complete reference of all available operations.\n"
msgstr "Le protocole comprend des opérations arithmétiques (comme l'addition, la multiplication et la composition), des opérations structurelles (itération sur les termes, analyse du support des modes, réétiquetage), des fonctions mathématiques (ordre normal, simplification, vérification d'équivalence), et plus encore. Consulte la documentation de [`OperatorTrait`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait\") pour une référence complète de toutes les opérations disponibles.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    ```python\n"
"    >>> from qiskit_fermions.operators import FermionOperator, cre, ann\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Construct a Hermitian operator: H = +0 -1 + +1 -0\n"
"    >>> op = FermionOperator.from_dict({\n"
"    ...     (cre(0), ann(1)): 1.0,\n"
"    ...     (cre(1), ann(0)): 1.0\n"
"    ... })\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Check if the operator is Hermitian\n"
"    >>> print(f\"Operator is Hermitian: {op.is_hermitian(atol=1e-10)}\")\n"
"    Operator is Hermitian: True\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Construct a Hermitian operator: H = +0 -1 + +1 -0\n"
"    QkComplex64 coeffs[2] = {{1.0, 0.0}, {1.0, 0.0}};\n"
"    bool actions[4] = {true, false, true, false};\n"
"    uint32_t modes[4] = {0, 1, 1, 0};\n"
"    uint32_t boundaries[3] = {0, 2, 4};\n"
"    QfFermionOperator *op = qf_ferm_op_new(2, 4, coeffs, actions, modes, boundaries);\n"
msgstr ""
"    // Construct a Hermitian operator: H = +0 -1 + +1 -0\n"
"    QkComplex64 coeffs[2] = {{1.0, 0.0}, {1.0, 0.0}};\n"
"    bool actions[4] = {true, false, true, false};\n"
"    uint32_t modes[4] = {0, 1, 1, 0};\n"
"    uint32_t boundaries[3] = {0, 2, 4};\n"
"    QfFermionOperator *op = qf_ferm_op_new(2, 4, coeffs, actions, modes, boundaries);\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Check if the operator is Hermitian\n"
"    bool is_hermitian = qf_ferm_op_is_hermitian(op, 1e-10);\n"
"    printf(\"Operator is Hermitian: %s\\n\", is_hermitian ? \"true\" : \"false\");\n"
msgstr ""
"    // Check if the operator is Hermitian\n"
"    bool is_hermitian = qf_ferm_op_is_hermitian(op, 1e-10);\n"
"    printf(\"Operator is Hermitian: %s\\n\", is_hermitian ? \"true\" : \"false\");\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Clean up\n"
"    qf_ferm_op_free(op);\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
"</Tabs>\n"
msgstr ""
"    // Clean up\n"
"    qf_ferm_op_free(op);\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
"</Tabs>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<DoqAdmonition type=\"note\" title=\"Note\">\n"
msgstr "<DoqAdmonition type=\"note\" title=\"Remarque\">\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "  [`is_hermitian()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian\") verifies that $H - H^\\dagger = 0$ by reducing that difference to a normal form and comparing it against [`zero()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.zero \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.zero\"). Spelled out in terms of the other protocol operations, it performs the following:\n"
msgstr "  [`is_hermitian()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian\") vérifie que $H - H^\\dagger = 0$ en réduisant cette différence à une forme normale et en la comparant à [`zero()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.zero \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.zero\"). Détaillée en termes des autres opérations du protocole, elle effectue les étapes suivantes :\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "  The strength of this check depends on the operator type, because it depends on how completely that type’s normal form contracts adjacent generators. A `True` result is always reliable, but for some types a `False` result is conservative: see [`is_hermitian()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian\") for details.\n"
msgstr "  La force de cette vérification dépend du type d'opérateur, car elle dépend de la complétude avec laquelle la forme normale de ce type contracte les générateurs adjacents. Un résultat `True` est toujours fiable, mais pour certains types un résultat `False` est prudent : consulte [`is_hermitian()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian\") pour plus de détails.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "</DoqAdmonition>\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Important\" type=\"danger\">\n"
"  The example uses `atol=1e-10`. The `atol` (absolute tolerance) parameter specifies a threshold. Coefficients with magnitude smaller than `atol` are treated as zero and discarded. This is essential for numerical stability when comparing operators, since floating-point arithmetic can introduce small rounding errors that would otherwise prevent equivalent operators from being recognized as such.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""
"<Admonition title=\"Important\" type=\"danger\">\n"
"  L'exemple utilise `atol=1e-10`. Le paramètre `atol` (tolérance absolue) précise un seuil. Les coefficients dont la magnitude est inférieure à `atol` sont traités comme nuls et supprimés. C'est essentiel pour la stabilité numérique lors de la comparaison d'opérateurs, car l'arithmétique à virgule flottante peut introduire de petites erreurs d'arrondi qui empêcheraient sinon de reconnaître des opérateurs équivalents comme tels.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Hint\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  While the [`OperatorTrait`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait\") protocol provides a common interface, individual operator implementations might offer additional convenience methods not part of the protocol. For example, [`FermionOperator`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-fermion-operator#qiskit_fermions.operators.FermionOperator \"qiskit_fermions.operators.FermionOperator\") and [`MajoranaOperator`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-majorana-operator#qiskit_fermions.operators.MajoranaOperator \"qiskit_fermions.operators.MajoranaOperator\") provide a `max_rank()` method that reports the length of their longest term. Always consult the API documentation for your operator type to discover all available functionality.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""
"<Admonition title=\"Astuce\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  Bien que le protocole [`OperatorTrait`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait\") fournisse une interface commune, les implémentations individuelles d'opérateurs peuvent offrir des méthodes pratiques supplémentaires qui ne font pas partie du protocole. Par exemple, [`FermionOperator`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-fermion-operator#qiskit_fermions.operators.FermionOperator \"qiskit_fermions.operators.FermionOperator\") et [`MajoranaOperator`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-majorana-operator#qiskit_fermions.operators.MajoranaOperator \"qiskit_fermions.operators.MajoranaOperator\") fournissent une méthode `max_rank()` qui indique la longueur de leur terme le plus long. Consulte toujours la documentation de l'API de ton type d'opérateur pour découvrir toutes les fonctionnalités disponibles.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"operator-term-ordering\" />\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Operator term ordering and normal forms {#operator-term-ordering-and-normal-forms}"
msgstr "Ordonnancement des termes d'un opérateur et formes normales {#operator-term-ordering-and-normal-forms}"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A fundamental challenge in quantum operator algebra is that mathematically equivalent operators can be represented in many different ways, each with different implications for quantum algorithms. The same operator can be written in algebraically equivalent forms (for example, $a^\\dagger b$ can be expressed as $ba^\\dagger + [a^\\dagger,b]$), yet these representations lead to different behavior in circuit synthesis, simplification, and numerical algorithms.\n"
msgstr "Un défi fondamental de l'algèbre des opérateurs quantiques est que des opérateurs mathématiquement équivalents peuvent être représentés de nombreuses façons différentes, chacune ayant des implications différentes pour les algorithmes quantiques. Un même opérateur peut s'écrire sous des formes algébriquement équivalentes (par exemple, $a^\\dagger b$ peut s'exprimer comme $ba^\\dagger + [a^\\dagger,b]$), et pourtant ces représentations conduisent à des comportements différents dans la synthèse de circuits, la simplification et les algorithmes numériques.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The operator representations support **normal ordering** operations that transform operators into algebra-specific canonical forms using commutation relations. This enables reliable comparisons (two equivalent operators have identical normal-ordered forms), reveals simplifications (commutation relations cause terms to cancel or combine), and supports algorithms that require specific operator forms for correctness and efficiency.\n"
msgstr "Les représentations d'opérateurs prennent en charge des opérations d'**ordre normal** qui transforment les opérateurs en formes canoniques propres à chaque algèbre en utilisant les relations de commutation. Cela permet des comparaisons fiables (deux opérateurs équivalents ont des formes en ordre normal identiques), révèle des simplifications (les relations de commutation font s'annuler ou se combiner des termes) et prend en charge les algorithmes qui exigent des formes d'opérateurs spécifiques pour leur exactitude et leur efficacité.\n"

# doq: translated after an English change 2026-11-13
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Important\" type=\"danger\">\n"
"  Different operator representations might implement normal ordering based on different commutation relations appropriate to their algebra. For example, fermionic normal ordering uses anticommutation relations ($\\{c_i, c_j^\\dagger\\} = \\delta_{ij}$), while Majorana normal ordering uses different algebra conventions ($\\{\\gamma_i, \\gamma_j\\} = 2\\delta_{ij}$). Always consult the documentation for your operator type to understand how normal ordering is implemented.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""
"<Admonition title=\"Important\" type=\"danger\">\n"
"  Différentes représentations d'opérateurs peuvent implémenter l'ordre normal à partir de relations de commutation différentes, adaptées à leur algèbre. Par exemple, l'ordre normal fermionique utilise des relations d'anticommutation ($\\{c_i, c_j^\\dagger\\} = \\delta_{ij}$), tandis que l'ordre normal de Majorana utilise d'autres conventions algébriques ($\\{\\gamma_i, \\gamma_j\\} = 2\\delta_{ij}$). Consulte toujours la documentation de ton type d'opérateur pour comprendre comment l'ordre normal est implémenté.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    ```python\n"
"    >>> from qiskit_fermions.operators import FermionOperator, cre, ann\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Two different representations of the same operator\n"
"    >>> op1 = FermionOperator.from_dict({(ann(0), cre(0)): 1.0})\n"
"    >>> op2 = FermionOperator.from_dict({(): 1.0, (cre(0), ann(0)): -1.0})\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Direct comparison fails due to different forms\n"
"    >>> op1.equiv(op2, atol=1e-10)\n"
"    False\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Normal-order both and compare again\n"
"    >>> op1_normal = op1.normal_ordered()\n"
"    >>> op2_normal = op2.normal_ordered()\n"
"    >>> op1_normal.equiv(op2_normal, atol=1e-10)\n"
"    True\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    ```c\n"
"    #include <qiskit_fermions.h>\n"
"    #include <stdbool.h>\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Two different representations of the same operator\n"
"    QkComplex64 coeff1[1] = {{1.0, 0.0}};\n"
"    bool actions1[2] = {false, true};\n"
"    uint32_t modes1[2] = {0, 0};\n"
"    uint32_t boundaries1[2] = {0, 2};\n"
"    QfFermionOperator *op1 = qf_ferm_op_new(1, 2, coeff1, actions1, modes1, boundaries1);\n"
msgstr ""
"    // Two different representations of the same operator\n"
"    QkComplex64 coeff1[1] = {{1.0, 0.0}};\n"
"    bool actions1[2] = {false, true};\n"
"    uint32_t modes1[2] = {0, 0};\n"
"    uint32_t boundaries1[2] = {0, 2};\n"
"    QfFermionOperator *op1 = qf_ferm_op_new(1, 2, coeff1, actions1, modes1, boundaries1);\n"

